 {"id":"fabro-18a5","title":"Terminal soft-exit edges classify runs as succeeded, blocking resume in CLI and web UI","status":"open","type":"bug","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-03T05:48:55.539Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-04T06:33:15.658Z","description":"\n\nLIVE REPRODUCTION 2026-09-04 (run 01M1NGGDVKNJHWP2W0HPJJ84CZ, first merged-world develop run): the gate-deadlock guard fired correctly after 3 gate crashes, the exit stage completed 'succeeded', and the run terminal status became succeeded(completed). Consequences observed: (a) the PUBLISH PATH RAN — PR #14 opened with auto-merge for code whose clippy/nextest never ran (closed manually, unmerged); (b) the deadlock semantics (work preserved, human decides, seeds stay open) were correct on the branch but invisible in the terminal status. This escalates the severity: soft/deadlock exits must classify as failed(SoftStop/Deadlock) BEFORE publish eligibility, not just for resume UX. Evidence: docker log 06:30:53 edge 'Gate deadlock (3 reds, needs a human)' -> exit succeeded; PR #14 created 06:31:11.","labels":["needs-triage"]}
 {"id":"fabro-83af","title":"Graph-level retry policy with backoff config and node-level override for stage retries","status":"open","type":"feature","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-03T05:49:01.421Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-03T05:49:01.421Z","description":"Stage retries today (lib/components/fabro-workflow/src/retry.rs) support node attributes 'retry_policy' (presets: none/standard/aggressive/linear/patient) and 'max_retries', plus a single graph attribute 'default_max_retries' that sets only the attempt count. The backoff shape is hardcoded (initial 5s, factor 2.0, cap 60s, jitter) and presets can only be selected per node.\n\nGap: transient provider errors (e.g. zai 429 rate limits) can hit ANY LLM node in a workflow, so the retry default belongs at workflow level. Today every LLM node repeats 'retry_policy=standard' manually (see revisor graph nodes select/analyze/file in run 01M1HKRE686W833SE86D8WD319); forgetting one node silently leaves it without retries.\n\nDemand: declare a retry policy once at graph level (preset name, and optionally explicit backoff attributes initial_delay/factor/max_delay/jitter), applying to all nodes. Node attributes override the workflow default for special cases. Precedence: node retry_policy > node max_retries > graph retry_policy/backoff > graph default_max_retries > none. Exponential backoff with jitter already exists (fabro_util::backoff) and should stay the default shape.","labels":["needs-triage"]}
 {"id":"fabro-a3d8","title":"Long-window rate limits defeat count-based retries: honor provider reset times or park-and-resume","status":"open","type":"feature","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-03T05:49:08.872Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-03T05:49:08.872Z","description":"Evidence: run 01M1HKRE686W833SE86D8WD319 burned its full stage retry budget (5 attempts, exponential backoff 5s..40s, ~2 min total) against a zai 429 whose usage window reset only hours later ('Usage limit reached for 5 hour. Your limit will reset at 2026-09-03 05:35:16'). The provider sent retry_after=null; the reset timestamp exists only inside the error message text. Three nested retry layers (agent.llm.retry x3, stage retries x5) all exhaust quickly against a multi-hour window, then the run soft-exits.\n\nCount-based exponential backoff cannot bridge multi-hour provider windows: the delays are far too short and the attempt count exhausts within minutes while the limit stays.\n\nFix directions to evaluate: (1) parse provider reset/retry-after hints (header AND message text) and cap or extend retry delays accordingly; (2) a wall-clock retry budget option (retry until T) instead of/in addition to attempt counts; (3) park-and-resume semantics: on a long-window 429, park the run in a resumable state with a scheduled wake-up instead of exhausting retries and exiting. Related: fabro-18a5 (soft-exit runs cannot be resumed), fabro-83af (graph-level retry defaults).","labels":["needs-triage"]}
